      "knowledge_point_id": "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function",
      "problem": "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=-tan⁡x$ when $x=\\pi .$",
      "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change  when $x=\\pi $ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-tan⁡x) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\pi ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-{sec}^{2}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate of change is $-1.$",
